IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `fpa-dates`
IMPORT `fpa-rules`

-- Family Provision Act 1969 (ACT) -- the questions of the beneficiary who answers a family provision claim.
--
-- SEAT: the individual who takes under the will or on intestacy (a legatee, devisee or other beneficiary) and whose share
-- may be cut to pay for provision ordered for a family member. "Can I apply to have the order reduced or discharged?",
-- "Whom must I serve?", "How much of the provision comes out of my share?", "Can property already handed to me be
-- taken?", "Can I be ordered to pay a lump sum instead?". A second seat beside the claimant's (fpa-claimant.l4): it draws
-- on the same shared rules (fpa-rules.l4) and defines none of the claimant module's names.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: s 9A (1), (2), (4) with s 10 (1) (applying to reduce, suspend or discharge an order; service),
-- s 11 (2), (3) (the burden of the provision), s 15 (1) (paying a lump sum and being exonerated), s 20 (property already
-- distributed, the mirror of the claimant's question). Out, and why: NOTES.md.
--
-- Edition: Family Provision Act 1969, A1969-15, Republication No 12 (R12), effective 12 December 2023, deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/A1969-15.txt.
--
-- (c) Australian Capital Territory. Used under CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md). Unofficial text.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Judgments the Act hands to the Supreme Court are INPUTS. Answers
-- follow the rulings on negatives of 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05 (NOTES.md).

§ `Family Provision Act 1969: the questions of the beneficiary answering a claim`

§§ `ss 9A, 10 -- reducing, suspending or discharging an order`

-- s 9A (1): "previous order means an order made under this Act that has not been discharged."
-- s 9A (2): "on application made by or on behalf of the administrator ... or a person beneficially entitled to, or having
-- an interest in, a part of the estate ..., the Supreme Court may, in its discretion ..., by order -- (a) vary a previous
-- order ... by reducing the amount of the provision ...; or (b) suspend a previous order ... for a specified period; or (c)
-- discharge a previous order".
--   JUST TRUE  -- a beneficiary, and a previous order exists: the application can be made (the outcome is the court's);
--   JUST FALSE -- no previous order (the "means" definition: nothing to vary);
--   NOTHING    -- not beneficially entitled or interested: the grant is limited in its own wording (ruling (c)).
@export Can I apply to the ACT Supreme Court to reduce, suspend or discharge a family provision order made out of an estate I have a share in (Family Provision Act 1969 s 9A (2))? Yes, if you are beneficially entitled to, or have an interest in, part of the estate and there is an order under the Act that has not been discharged. Whether the court reduces, suspends or discharges it is for the court.
GIVEN `I have a share` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you beneficially entitled to, or do you have an interest in, part of the deceased's estate (under the will or on intestacy)?
      `an order stands` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is there a family provision order made under the Act out of this estate that has not been discharged?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 9A (2) -- can I apply to cut a provision order` `I have a share` `an order stands` MEANS
    IF (NOT `an order stands`)
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE IF `I have a share`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE NOTHING

DECLARE `Person to serve` IS ONE OF
    `the person in whose favour the order was made (s 9A (4))`
    `each administrator of the estate (s 10 (1))`

-- s 9A (4): "The applicant for an order under subsection (2) shall cause notice of the application to be served on the
-- person in whose favour the previous order was made." s 10 (1): the applicant for an order under s 9A "shall cause notice
-- of the application to be served on each person who is an administrator". No time is set for either.
-- Fork F5 (a duty whose "if" is unmet): no application, no one to serve (an empty list).
@export Whom must I serve with notice of my application to reduce, suspend or discharge a family provision order (Family Provision Act 1969 ss 9A (4), 10 (1))? The person the order was made for, and each administrator of the estate. The court may order service on others too.
GIVEN `applied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you applied to the Supreme Court to reduce, suspend or discharge the order?
GIVETH A LIST OF `Person to serve`
`ss 9A, 10 -- whom must I serve` `applied` MEANS
    IF `applied`
    THEN LIST `the person in whose favour the order was made (s 9A (4))`, `each administrator of the estate (s 10 (1))`
    ELSE EMPTY

§§ `s 11 -- who bears the provision`

-- s 11 (2): "Unless the Supreme Court otherwise orders, the burden of the provision ordered ... shall, subject to
-- subsection (3), be borne between the persons beneficially entitled to the estate ... (other than the person or persons
-- in whose favour an order or orders under this Act is or are made), in proportion to the values of their respective
-- interests in the estate."
-- s 11 (3): successive interests in property settled by the will are not valued separately; their proportion is "raised
-- or charged against the corpus of that property".
--   JUST n     -- my share of the burden: provision x my interest / all the bearing interests;
--   JUST 0     -- an order under the Act was made in my favour (fork F9, "(other than X)": I bear none);
--   NOTHING    -- the court has ordered otherwise (its order decides); my interest is one of successive interests under a
--                 settlement in the will (s 11 (3): the settled property bears its proportion as a whole, charged on the
--                 corpus, not interest by interest); or the bearing interests are worth nothing.
@export How much of the provision ordered for a family member comes out of my share of the estate (Family Provision Act 1969 s 11 (2), (3))? Unless the court orders otherwise, the provision is borne by the beneficiaries (other than anyone the Act's orders were made for) in proportion to the values of their interests. Successive interests in property settled by the will are not valued separately: that property's part is charged on the property itself.
GIVEN `the provision` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount (in dollars) of the provision ordered out of the estate
      `my interest` IS A NUMBER @desc The value (in dollars) of your interest in the estate
      `all the bearing interests` IS A NUMBER @desc The total value (in dollars) of the interests of all the people beneficially entitled to the estate, yours included, leaving out anyone an order under the Act was made for
      `an order for me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was an order under the Act made in your favour?
      `the court ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Supreme Court ordered a different way of bearing the provision?
      `a settled successive interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your interest one of successive interests (for example a life interest followed by a remainder) in property settled by the will?
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 11 -- my share of the burden` `the provision` `my interest` `all the bearing interests` `an order for me` `the court ordered otherwise` `a settled successive interest` MEANS
    IF `the court ordered otherwise`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `an order for me`
         THEN JUST 0
         ELSE IF `a settled successive interest` OR `all the bearing interests` AT MOST 0
              THEN NOTHING
              ELSE JUST ((`the provision` TIMES `my interest`) DIVIDED BY `all the bearing interests`)

§§ `ss 15, 20 -- paying instead, and property already distributed`

-- s 15 (1): "The Supreme Court may, when making, or at any time after having made, an order under section 8 or 9A, order a
-- person who is entitled to a share in the estate ... as a legatee, devisee or beneficiary to pay a lump sum or periodical
-- payments ... to represent, or in commutation of, a proportion of the provision ... that falls on the legatee, devisee or
-- beneficiary, and may exonerate the property or a specified part of the property to which the legatee, devisee or
-- beneficiary is entitled from further liability".
--   JUST TRUE -- an order under s 8 or 9A is being or has been made and I take a share: the court may so order;
--   NOTHING   -- otherwise: the power is limited in its own wording (ruling (c)).
@export Can the ACT Supreme Court order me to pay a lump sum or periodical payments in place of my part of the provision, and free my share from further liability (Family Provision Act 1969 s 15)? It may, when making a family provision order or at any time after, if you are entitled to a share of the estate as a legatee, devisee or beneficiary. It may also direct how the payment is secured, to whom it is paid and how it is invested.
GIVEN `I take a share` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you entitled to a share in the estate as a legatee, devisee or beneficiary?
      `an order is made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the court making, or has it made, a family provision order (under s 8 or s 9A) out of this estate?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 15 -- can I be ordered to pay instead` `I take a share` `an order is made` MEANS
    IF `I take a share` AND `an order is made` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE NOTHING

-- s 20, from the beneficiary's seat: can property distributed to me be used for the provision? The same rule as the
-- claimant's question (fpa-rules.l4), so the two seats agree.
@export Can the ACT Supreme Court direct that a family provision be made out of property already distributed to me from the estate (Family Provision Act 1969 s 20)? Yes, unless the distribution was properly made for the maintenance, education or advancement of someone who depended on the deceased, or it was made more than 6 months after the grant, before the administrator had notice of the claim (or of an application for more time to claim), and the property has vested in possession.
GIVEN `made for a dependant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the distribution to you properly made to provide for the proper maintenance, education or advancement in life of someone (you or another) who was wholly or partly dependent on the deceased immediately before the death?
      `the grant` IS A DATE @desc The date administration of the estate was granted (or sealed in the ACT), e.g. 2026-03-10
      `distributed on` IS A DATE @desc The date the property was distributed to you
      `notice first` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Before the distribution, had the administrator had notice of the family provision application (or of an application to extend the time to make one)?
      `vested` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the property vested in possession (handed over to you, or to whoever now has it)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 20 -- can property distributed to me be taken for the provision` `made for a dependant` `the grant` `distributed on` `notice first` `vested` MEANS
    IF `s 20 (2) bars the distributed property` `made for a dependant` `the grant` `distributed on` `notice first` `vested`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE JUST TRUE
